---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const title = 'Keeping patient data out of code · Kenechi Ucheagwu';
const description = 'Open source with synthetic data: git hooks and a full-history scan that keep patient names and IDs out of code, without the scanner holding a readable patient list.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="/projects/phi-guardrails/">
  <header class="page-header page-header--lean">
    <div class="shell">
      <h1>Keeping patient data out of code</h1>
      <p class="work-label work-label--demo">Open source, synthetic data</p>
      <p class="case-lede">Git hooks and a full-history scan that block patient names and IDs before they reach a repository, without the guard itself holding a readable patient list. It can't catch every case, and it says so.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <article class="article article--lean shell">
    <section class="article-section" id="problem">
      <h2><span class="section-count" aria-hidden="true">01</span><span>The problem</span></h2>
      <div>
        <p>Scripts written around a medical office collect names, birth dates and member IDs over time. A script fixes one stuck claim, a test copies one patient's details, an export lands next to the code "for a minute". Deleting the file later doesn't help, because git keeps every old version.</p>
        <p>A scanner that knew real patient names would be its own patient list, which is the leak you were trying to prevent.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="article-section" id="constraints">
      <h2><span class="section-count" aria-hidden="true">02</span><span>Constraints</span></h2>
      <ul>
        <li>It never holds a readable patient list.</li>
        <li>Findings never print the matched value.</li>
        <li>It fails closed: a missing or damaged roster blocks the commit.</li>
        <li>The repository uses synthetic data only.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="article-section" id="built">
      <h2><span class="section-count" aria-hidden="true">03</span><span>What I built</span></h2>
      <ul>
        <li>A roster of HMAC-SHA256 digests of patient names, member IDs and name plus birth date, built from EHR exports and kept outside the repository. Without the key, a copy of the roster doesn't reveal who is on it.</li>
        <li>Pre-commit, pre-merge-commit and commit-msg hooks that check the lines being added and the commit message.</li>
        <li>A history scan that reads every past version of every file, and also reads Word, Excel and PowerPoint files and PDFs.</li>
        <li>Pattern and secret checks for what a roster can't know, such as Social Security numbers, record numbers and cloud keys.</li>
        <li>Binaries such as spreadsheets and screenshots blocked by default, unless someone reviews and allows them.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="article-section" id="result">
      <h2><span class="section-count" aria-hidden="true">04</span><span>Result</span></h2>
      <p>140 tests pass on Ubuntu and Windows across three Python versions. The tests build their roster from invented people at runtime, and the repository's own CI runs the history scan on itself. None of the practice's code or data is in it.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="article-section" id="limits">
      <h2><span class="section-count" aria-hidden="true">05</span><span>Limits</span></h2>
      <p>Hooks can be bypassed, and patterns miss things. The roster only knows patients from the exports it was built from. PDF reading needs an optional extra. It is a guardrail for developers, not a compliance program.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="article-section" id="links">
      <h2><span class="section-count" aria-hidden="true">06</span><span>Links</span></h2>
      <div class="link-row" aria-label="Project links">
        <a href="https://github.com/kenechi-ucheagwu/phi-guardrails">Code on GitHub</a>
        <a href="https://github.com/kenechi-ucheagwu/phi-guardrails#quick-start">Quick start</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>
